    benchmark_summary: "Subnet 2 (on-chain name 'DSperse', formerly Omron) is Inference Labs' zkML proving network: validators dispatch inference jobs, miners return predictions plus zero-knowledge proofs, and scoring is based on proof validity, proof size and latency. The stack is now native Rust (sn2-miner/sn2-validator over HTTP and QUIC) and is the most actively developed of the four by a wide margin \u2014 daily commits through 2026-08-12, 9 contributors, releases in the 14.13.x series. The commercial layer is real and live: Sertn (sertn.ai), a verifiable computer-vision SaaS built on DSperse's slice-based proving, with a working dashboard, SSO, API keys (ilk_ prefix), an MCP server at api.sertn.ai/mcp, docs, and a published price list \u2014 Free $0 (3 projects, 10K images), Team $99/month per seat (hosted training, ZK proofs, up to 1K proof artifacts/month), Enterprise custom (self-hosted/edge, SLAs, SSO/RBAC). Supporting open-source is substantial: DSperse, JSTprove, an Expander/GKR fork, agent-kit. Marketing targets aviation, industrial inspection, healthcare imaging and energy under EU AI Act framing. Chain shows 7 validators, 14 active miners, emissions flowing, network-wide proof counters at 950M+ on the corporate site (300M+ on the subnet docs). AUDIT: Best-in-set on openness: MIT licensed, code public across subnet-2, dsperse, JSTprove and the Expander fork, real multi-contributor history. Notable risks all the same. (1) Supply chain: miners and validators run pre-built binaries with a built-in auto-updater that polls Inference Labs' GitHub releases every 5 minutes and atomically replaces the running binary \u2014 a compromise of that release pipeline propagates network-wide within minutes, and the install path is a curl-pipe-to-bash script. (2) Validator concentration: 7 validators over 256 keys, with the owner hotkey 5CFxLBvpyQq3TCP7zLcu8dLcPBxhA6MdLvXiCyvJVojuK17J and owner coldkey 5EcYQ3W77ndrmMWdvVQusoFqY8doxfP3U2zrh7xZQiaz7avY controlling spec/weights-version bumps. (3) Sertn itself is a fully centralised hosted SaaS \u2014 customer trust in the proofs is cryptographic, but availability and key custody are not decentralised. (4) I found NO third-party security or cryptographic audit of JSTprove, DSperse or the Expander fork, which is a material gap for a project selling cryptographic assurance into aviation and medical imaging. CAVEATS: Zero paying customers are named, so all industry targeting (aviation, defense, healthcare) is currently positioning, not proven demand \u2014 do not read the use-case marketing as customers. The 'Enterprise' tier is a book-a-demo motion with no evidence of a closed deal. I could not verify the 950M proofs figure independently, and note the corporate site (950M) and subnet docs (300M+) disagree, so at least one is stale. GitHub star counts across this org are anomalously high relative to contributor count and Bittensor norms (subnet-2 2,376; dsperse 1,271; JSTprove 1,252 versus 12 for the NATIX subnet repo) \u2014 I could not determine whether that reflects genuine zkML interest or promotion, so I gave stars no weight in the score. Finally, revenue that Sertn earns accrues to Inference Labs the company; do not assume it flows to alpha holders of subnet 2.",
