    benchmark_summary: "SILX AI runs subnet 24 as a decentralized continued-pretraining run for its own 'Quasar' long-context MoE language models. A subnet-operated orchestrator splits the model's trainable tensors into 24 fragments, leases fragments to miners to train, pulls live fragment states, has validators verify signed claims and tensor hashes, merges only validator-approved fragments, and periodically publishes updated checkpoints. The stated goal is 10T+ additional pretraining tokens on 'Quasar Preview'. What is genuinely live and verifiable is the open-weight model release on HuggingFace: silx-ai/Quasar-10B (4,220 downloads, 52 likes, Apache-2.0), silx-ai/Quasar-Preview (1,343 downloads, 95 likes, MIT), Quasar-3B-A1B-Preview (232 downloads). There is no inference API, no hosted product, no console and no pricing page anywhere on silxinc.com. The buyer today is a researcher downloading free weights, not a paying customer. AUDIT: QUASAR-SUBNET is MIT licensed and public, which is good. Decentralisation posture is weak by design and the README is candid about it: 'The orchestrator is run by the subnet operator' \u2014 a single SILX-operated service assigns all work, merges all accepted updates, and releases all checkpoints. Miners and validators cannot assign work or merge; they only train and verify. That orchestrator is an unambiguous single point of failure and a single point of control over what enters the model. Mitigating design details do exist and are real: signed manifests with scoped grants rather than broad operator credentials, tensor-contract and hash verification, rejection of unsigned/stale/replayed claims, and decay-weighted scoring so old accepted work cannot dominate. No third-party security audit exists or is claimed. Contributor concentration is extreme: the subnet repo has exactly ONE contributor (ahmed3520, 6 commits) \u2014 this is a one-person codebase. Key custody for validators is not documented publicly. CAVEATS: The most important thing I found, and it undercuts the 'novel architecture' pitch: silx-ai/Quasar-10B's own repository files are configuration_qwen3_5.py and modeling_qwen3_5.py \u2014 i.e. the flagship 10B model is built on a Qwen 3.5 architecture, not the bespoke 'Quasar Attention / Token Temperature' architecture the marketing emphasises. Anyone underwriting this on the strength of proprietary architecture claims should verify that themselves before allocating. Second concern: the entire subnet repo is 6 commits from a single author, and the last push was 2026-06-26 \u2014 roughly seven weeks stale at time of review \u2014 with commit messages ('Prepare Quasar subnet production release') suggesting production launch was recent and lightly staffed. Third: I found no independent benchmark results, no third-party evaluation, and no published eval numbers substantiating the 'crush the long-context barrier' claim; download counts in the low thousands are modest for a model claiming frontier long-context capability. I could NOT review the X account (@QuasarModels) or any founder statements, podcasts, or funding announcements \u2014 web search budget was exhausted and the X API credential available was rejected \u2014 so if revenue or a raise was announced verbally somewhere, I did not see it. The 10T-token training goal is aspiration with no published progress metric I could verify.",
    active_users: "https://silxinc.com/ \u2014 the site is positioned as a 'frontier research lab' shipping open-weight models with 'full weights under Apache 2.0'; there is no pricing page, no API product, no customer logos and no billing surface anywhere on the site or in the subnet repo. All distribution is free downloads via https://huggingface.co/silx-ai. Giving away Apache-2.0/MIT weights with no hosted endpoint is structurally pre-revenue.",
