    cost_saving_pct: 0,
    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Perturb is a decentralized adversarial-robustness network. A team-run task generator publishes a challenge image sampled from ImageNet-100; miners poll that task API, run PGD-style attacks to produce an imperceptible perturbation that flips a fixed EfficientNetV2-L classifier, upload the perturbed image and submit its URL; validators re-run the classifier, verify the perturbation norm and set on-chain weights. What is verifiably live today is the incentive mechanism itself (the subnet is emitting, 11 active miners, 3 validators, code shipping almost daily) plus a marketing site with a whitepaper and a 'playground' page. The two things it intends to sell \u2014 an adversarial-training dataset subscription and a model-robustness testing service with 'robustness certificates' pitched at EU AI Act conformity \u2014 are roadmap only. There is no pricing page with actual dollar figures, no customer, no self-serve signup, and the leaderboard URL returns 404. Note SN26 was re-registered from scratch on 2026-05-06 for ~1,504 TAO; the previous occupant (Kinitro) is gone, so any pre-May-2026 SN26 data is a different project. AUDIT: No third-party security audit of any kind. Code is public at github.com/0xsigurd/Perturb but carries NO LICENSE file, so it is source-available rather than formally open source. Severe key-person risk: a single contributor (0xsigurd, 131 of 131 commits) on a GitHub account with one public repo, no bio, no linked X \u2014 the four named people on perturbai.io (Koyuki Nakamori CEO, Jeffrey Lamb CTO, Vadym Shakuro, Vidusha Sanidu) do not map to any verifiable GitHub or public identity. Hard architectural centralisation: challenges are not generated by validators but by a team-operated task API, and miner submissions are uploaded to and read back from that same team API gated by PERTURB_API_KEY \u2014 the owner controls challenge issuance and the evidence store, and if that API stops, the subnet stops. Only 3 validators. weights_version is 0 on chain, and a cross-validator score-consensus mechanism was only merged on 2026-08-12, meaning weight setting was until this week not consensus-derived. On-chain owner coldkey 5FsVAEPMpkAo3eP7xTNJYLyBndi2oT5GPxVPzFT1DzSD82qx, owner hotkey 5EHfTi6RWouYP7YQD5tXgm56SgeC1Wp7xS7BVG6uCRWw3fvP. CAVEATS: The existing AlphaGap score of 58 is too high and should come down \u2014 nothing has been productised since it was set. I could not verify a single team member's identity independently; the names on the site are unbacked. I could not test the product: there is no public API, no demo output, no dataset sample and no published benchmark, so the claim that the network produces commercially useful adversarial examples is entirely unverified. The 'model robustness certificates relevant to EU AI Act conformity' framing is, in my judgement, materially overstated \u2014 no regulator or standards body recognises an on-chain attestation from an unaudited anonymous subnet, and there is no evidence any assessor has accepted one. 19 forks with only 3 stars is the signature of miners cloning to farm, not of developer interest. Repo is ~4 months old.",
    active_users: "https://www.perturbai.io/whitepaper \u2014 the whitepaper lists monetisation as forward-looking targets only: months 3-6 '$15,000 MRR', months 6-12 '$80,000 MRR', year 2 '$500,000+ ARR'. Subscription tiers (Research 100K examples/mo, Professional 1M/mo, Enterprise unlimited) are named but carry no prices. No customer is named anywhere on the site or in the repo, and https://www.perturbai.io/leaderboard returns HTTP 404. Trailing revenue is therefore zero.",
