    benchmark_summary: "A Bittensor subnet for moral-reasoning alignment of LLMs. Miners submit structured ethical-dilemma scenario configurations; validators score them through an 8-stage pipeline and run accepted scenarios through Google DeepMind's Concordia generative-agent simulation framework. The resulting transcripts form training data intended to improve LLM performance on moral-reasoning benchmarks (MoReBench). What is verifiably live today: the open-source validator/miner stack, public GHCR Docker images for validator, miner and Concordia sidecar, a mainnet (SN37) and testnet (SN455) deployment, and a whitepaper. Also live is a June 2026 activation-steering competition run jointly with Apex (subnet 1). The commercial product, 'Proba' \u2014 an interpretability platform that predicts what fine-tuning data will do to a model before you train \u2014 was announced in July 2026 as 'launching in August', so as of mid-August 2026 it is at or just past launch and has no disclosed users. There is no buyer-facing pricing anywhere on the site. AUDIT: Open source under MIT, and unusually well-documented for a subnet this size. Two material centralisation facts a buyer must know. First, the validator's mainnet defaults are DEFAULT_BURN_MODE = True and DEFAULT_BURN_PERCENTAGE = 1.0 (aurelius/common/constants.py), routing 100% of emissions to burn UID 200 rather than paying miners \u2014 a May 2026 commit explicitly flipped this on. The subnet is therefore not currently running a live miner payout economy by default. Second, validators pull operational config from a team-controlled 'Central API' hosted on Railway (new-collector-api-production.up.railway.app), which can remotely change burn mode, scoring thresholds and simulation parameters. That is a single point of failure and gives the subnet owner unilateral control over scoring. Only 2 contributors on the repo and 0 stars. No third-party security audit found. Mitigating: the advisory bench is genuinely credible \u2014 Steffen Cruz (former Opentensor Foundation CTO, Macrocosmos founder), Prof. Robert West (EPFL), AE Studio, and crypto counsel Ryon Nixon; co-founders Austin McCaffrey (CEO) and Coleman Maher (COO, ex-Parity/Origin Protocol) are publicly named. CAVEATS: The existing benchmark_score of 52 is too generous and should come down. The existing recorded revenue of $0 is correct. I could not verify an official X handle or Discord invite for Aurelius from a primary source, so I have left those blank rather than guess. I could not reach a live Proba product page (aureliusaligned.ai/proba 404s), so I cannot confirm Proba actually shipped in August \u2014 the site's own overview claims users can 'select a model, training method, and dataset to get results within minutes', which I consider an overstated description of an unshipped or barely-shipped product. The 100% burn default is the single most important fact here and is not reflected in any public subnet directory I checked. WebSearch budget was exhausted for this session, so I could not sweep X/podcasts for founder revenue statements; my pre-revenue call rests on the absence of any pricing or customer disclosure across their own properties.",
    active_users: "https://aureliusaligned.ai/blog \u2014 the July 2026 post announces Proba as an interpretability platform 'launching in August'; no pricing page, no customer logos, and no revenue figure exists anywhere on aureliusaligned.ai, its team page or its blog. The only token of value exchange in the protocol is the miner-side WORK_TOKEN_COST of 1.0 alpha per submission, which is a Sybil-resistance cost paid in alpha, not external revenue.",
    annual_revenue_usd: 0,
    revenue_confidence: "confirmed_pre_revenue",
    revenue_evidence: "https://aureliusaligned.ai/blog \u2014 the July 2026 post announces Proba as an interpretability platform 'launching in August'; no pricing page, no customer logos, and no revenue figure exists anywhere on aureliusaligned.ai, its team page or its blog. The only token of value exchange in the protocol is the miner-side WORK_TOKEN_COST of 1.0 alpha per submission, which is a Sybil-resistance cost paid in alpha, not external revenue.",
