    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Subnet 48 is qBitTensor Labs' decentralised quantum-compute network: miners are not conventional GPU operators but parties who own, operate or are formally partnered with owners of real quantum computers, and they sell spare QPU capacity into the network. Demand is routed through Open Quantum (openquantum.com), a live commercial platform that gives developers access to real QPUs from IonQ, Rigetti, IQM and AQT through a single API. Open Quantum offers two execution paths: 'Public Compute', which routes jobs through qBitTensor Labs and the subnet at half the cost of direct access, and 'Private Compute' on dedicated infrastructure. This is genuinely live and commercial, not roadmap \u2014 there is a published pricing table with per-task fees ($0.18 public / $0.36 private) and per-shot fees by backend (from $0.000255 for Rigetti Cepheus-1-108Q up to $0.048 for IonQ Forte), a two-tier credit system (free Spark Credits, $50 claimable every 90 days, versus purchasable Full Credits valid one year), and a documented enterprise tier with SSO/SAML/OIDC, SCIM provisioning, centralised B2B invoicing with POs and NET terms, and org-level managed credits. Users submit QASM files through the web portal or pip-install the SDK into existing Qiskit or PennyLane workflows. Paid credits have been purchasable since late November 2025. AUDIT: Subnet code is open source at github.com/qbittensor-labs/quantum-compute (non-fork). Subnet owner coldkey 5GNH5YMkcX8jEF1PukvxKafifcqz13jp18BT73jRL3AZc4Rc. On-chain: 13 active validators, 6 active miners, 256 registered keys, emission 560,197. No third-party security audit found. The dominant risk here is not code but structural dependency and centralisation: (a) the entire commercial demand surface \u2014 portal, billing, credits, enterprise contracts, customer relationships \u2014 is owned and operated by Quantum Rings, a third party, so subnet 48 does not control its own go-to-market and could in principle be disintermediated; (b) miner decentralisation is inherently near-impossible, since only entities with physical access to QPUs can mine, and there are fewer than 100 such systems on Earth by the project's own README, so 6 active miners may be close to the practical ceiling \u2014 this is a permanent structural concentration, not a growth problem; (c) hardware supply concentrates further into four vendors (IonQ, Rigetti, IQM, AQT), any of which is a single point of failure; (d) contributor bus-factor is 1, with entantglethis at 37 commits versus ShorShot 5, sirentangled 1, tensorwrangler 1; (e) the README states emissions began at a 90% burn with only 10% available to incentivise quantum compute, scaling up as demand and revenue grow, so the owner controls the emission ramp. Note also a CI workflow was deleted on 2026-07-07, weakening automated quality gates. CAVEATS: I am materially revising the existing record (score 50, revenue $0, last researched 2026-07-21) upward, because that record appears to have missed that a live paid commercial product exists \u2014 but I want to be explicit that I still could not confirm a single dollar of revenue reaching the subnet. Unverifiable claims on their materials: 'Thousands of users, from students to engineers at Fortune 500 companies' is a marketing assertion with zero named customers, no logos and no substantiation \u2014 treat it as unproven. The 'Uber of Quantum Computing' framing in the README is explicitly roadmap. The strongest counter-signal to the commercial story is that subnet-side engineering is nearly dormant: only 7 commits since mid-May 2026, and every one is dependency, CI or test maintenance (Bittensor SDK breaking-change fixes, pascalcase fixes, requirements cleanup) with no product feature work \u2014 the real development is happening in Quantum Rings' platform, which is closed and outside this subnet. Partnership announcements (qBraid on 2026-08-11, PennyLane on 2026-07-15, Rigetti Cepheus) are integrations, not disclosed revenue. A Black Friday '100% discount on all Open Quantum products' promotion is a further hint that paid conversion may be low. I did not score this above 85 precisely because there are no named paying customers and no disclosed figure.",
    active_users: "A paid channel is confirmed to exist but no dollar figure is disclosed anywhere and attribution to the subnet is unclear, so I am deliberately NOT claiming a number. Evidence that paid sales are live: https://openquantum.com/pricing publishes real USD pricing and a purchasable 'Full Credits' tier, and @QuantumSN48 announced on 2025-11-29 'Credits are now available for purchase on Open Quantum - powered by @qBitTensorLabs and Bittensor $TAO'. Evidence blocking an estimate: no usage, job-volume or customer-count numbers are published anywhere, so no arithmetic is possible. Crucially, https://openquantum.com/about states 'Open Quantum is a platform operated by Quantum Rings' \u2014 a separate company \u2014 with qBitTensor Labs supplying only the Public Compute path, so credit revenue accrues in the first instance to Quantum Rings, and what share (if any) reaches subnet 48 is undisclosed. This is why I recorded 0 with confidence unknown rather than estimating.",
    annual_revenue_usd: 0,
    revenue_confidence: "unknown",
    revenue_evidence: "A paid channel is confirmed to exist but no dollar figure is disclosed anywhere and attribution to the subnet is unclear, so I am deliberately NOT claiming a number. Evidence that paid sales are live: https://openquantum.com/pricing publishes real USD pricing and a purchasable 'Full Credits' tier, and @QuantumSN48 announced on 2025-11-29 'Credits are now available for purchase on Open Quantum - powered by @qBitTensorLabs and Bittensor $TAO'. Evidence blocking an estimate: no usage, job-volume or customer-count numbers are published anywhere, so no arithmetic is possible. Crucially, https://openquantum.com/about states 'Open Quantum is a platform operated by Quantum Rings' \u2014 a separate company \u2014 with qBitTensor Labs supplying only the Public Compute path, so credit revenue accrues in the first instance to Quantum Rings, and what share (if any) reaches subnet 48 is undisclosed. This is why I recorded 0 with confidence unknown rather than estimating.",
