    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "A sim-to-real robotics policy tournament on Isaac Sim 5.1 / Isaac Lab. Miners upload trained control policies for a published task (Spot navigation, Leatherback LiDAR maze, Franka pick-and-place, humanoid run/jump); validators pull them and score them inside ephemeral GPU Docker sandboxes with capabilities dropped, read-only mounts and an SNI-proxy network whitelist. Winner-takes-all per tournament cycle, and winning weights are open-sourced to a SimStore. This is genuinely live, not roadmap: their public Tournament API returns 17 tournaments run since 2026-02-19 (16 non-test), each with a winning hotkey and score. Ancillary pieces (EnvHub asset library, nepher-cli, account portal) are live; the Hackathon product is explicitly still on staging. There is no buyer \u2014 participation is free and the only money in the system is TAO emission. AUDIT: MIT licensed, code genuinely open (github.com/nepher-ai, 14 repos, most recent push 2026-08-12). No third-party security audit found. Real centralisation risks, and they are material: (1) both miners and validators must configure a Nepher-issued API key to participate at all \u2014 the team can revoke access; (2) all tournament state, agent uploads, scores and environment downloads route through two team-operated services, tournament-api.nepher.ai and envhub-api.nepher.ai \u2014 both single points of failure, both currently healthy (HTTP 200 on /health at 2026-08-12T19:11Z); (3) the tournament records carry winner_approved / winner_approved_by / winner_approved_at fields, i.e. a Nepher admin manually ratifies who wins before rewards flow. That is discretionary control over emission allocation. Sandbox isolation of untrusted miner code is well designed (caps dropped, no Docker socket, no wallet access, NET_ADMIN dropped before miner code runs). Bus factor is very low: 3 contributors on the main subnet repo, 1-3 on the satellite repos, dominated by a single account (akhenova). CAVEATS: Blunt version: this subnet is currently near-idle. On-chain right now only 2 of 256 neurons have non-zero incentive (11 validators), and the sole active tournament \u2014 task-humanoid-run-jump, started ~2026-08-10 \u2014 is flagged is_test:true with a test_whitelist of exactly 2 accounts. So the 2 earning miners are the team's own test accounts. The last genuine open competition started 2026-07-15. That is roughly four weeks of no real competition, which the marketing does not mention. The tournaments.nepher.ai host referenced in older metadata no longer resolves (NXDOMAIN). I could not verify the 'trusted by robotics teams worldwide' claim \u2014 no customer is named anywhere, and I consider it overstated. I could not verify the NVIDIA Inception backing independently of their own site. The prior benchmark_score of 64 is not defensible on current evidence; I have cut it to 50 because the machinery is real and tournaments genuinely ran, but traction is close to nil and the winner-approval gate is a real governance flaw.",
    active_users: "No pricing anywhere. nepher.ai offers only 'Get started free'; there is no pricing or plans page, no checkout, no customer logos (only an unnamed 'trusted by robotics teams worldwide' claim and NVIDIA Inception membership, which is a free program). The one endpoint literally named pricing \u2014 https://tournament-api.nepher.ai/api/v1/tournaments/pricing?subnet_uid=49 \u2014 is not product pricing, it returns FX conversion: {\"network\":\"finney\",\"subnet_uid\":49,\"alpha_to_tao\":0.006979202,\"tao_to_usd\":199.6}. Tournament entry is free to miners. Conclusion: zero external revenue.",
