    benchmark_summary: "Engy, built by Hanlin AI (founder Ning Ren, ex-Google AI engineer, who also runs SN11 TrajectoryRL), sells verified inference for frontier open-weight models. The pitch is cryptographic proof that the exact model you paid for is the model that ran \u2014 miners execute the full model and commit compact activation fingerprints (TOPLOC-style), validators randomly audit those commitments, and model weights are pinned via published Merkle roots. No trusted hardware/TEE required. The API is live and OpenAI-compatible: I called https://api.engy.ai/v1/models directly on 2026-08-12 and it returned four models serving with per-token pricing \u2014 deepseek-v4-flash-0731 ($0.045/$0.09 per 1M in/out), qwen3.6-35b-a3b ($0.045/$0.30), glm-5.2 ($0.68/$1.50), kimi-k3 ($1.50/$7.50). Billing is pay-as-you-go per token, no subscriptions, no minimums. Documented integrations with Claude Code, Cursor and Codex. The subnet got broad attention in late July 2026 when Ning showed Kimi K3 (2.8T params) running on 80 consumer RTX 5090s over standard Ethernet with official MXFP4 weights, at 20 tok/s rising to a reported 110 tok/s \u2014 the post passed 750K views on X. AUDIT: Open source at https://github.com/hanlinai/engy \u2014 but note there is NO LICENSE file declared on the repo, which is a real risk for anyone building on it. Repo created 2026-07-06, 83 commits, 3 contributors (Ning Ren, Andy Zhang, andy), 5 stars, 3 forks, last push 2026-07-29 \u2014 i.e. roughly two weeks stale as of 2026-08-12. Contains genuine miner and validator clients, Docker configs, model-pinning specs and runbooks; not a template fork. Trust model is the actual selling point and is cryptographic rather than hardware-based (activation fingerprints + random validator audits + Merkle-rooted weight pinning), which is a stronger posture than most inference subnets. Centralisation risks: Hanlin AI is the subnet owner (18% owner cut), the sole meaningful development team, and the operator of the api.engy.ai gateway \u2014 the paid product routes through one company-controlled endpoint, so the decentralised backend does not remove a single point of failure at the commercial front door. Provider onboarding is also gated through provider.engy.ai. No third-party security audit found. Community support runs through a Hanlin-operated Discord (discord.com/invite/ZgjS3XyRMB), not an independent channel. CAVEATS: Could not verify: any revenue, any customer, any token throughput, any funding, or the claimed 110 tok/s figure (single founder statement, not independently benchmarked). Could not verify what payment rails are actually accepted \u2014 the site says pay-as-you-go but never states card vs crypto vs TAO, and the docs do not either. The verification claim itself is unaudited: I found no third party who has independently confirmed that the TOPLOC-style fingerprints actually catch a substituted or quantized model in practice. This subnet is roughly five weeks old at the repo level, and the July hype (300% alpha move, 750K-view post) is attention, not traction \u2014 do not let it inflate the score. Also note the name collides in search with the French utility ENGIE, which pollutes automated research. PRICE VERIFIED 2026-08-17 against api.engy.ai/v1/models: 5 models serving. DeepSeek-V4-Flash at $0.045 in / $0.09 out per 1M tokens, against DeepInfra $0.10/$0.20 and DeepSeek's own official API at $0.14/$0.28 \u2014 and DeepSeek moved to peak/off-peak billing on 2026-08-16 at $0.44/$1.32 peak and $0.22/$0.66 off-peak, so Engy is now roughly 2.2x cheaper than DeepInfra and up to 10-15x cheaper than the model's own first-party API at peak. qwen3.6-35b-a3b and qwen3.8-27b also serve at $0.045 input. The cheapest-inference claim is real and widened materially in the last 48 hours. What still holds the score below 90: no disclosed revenue and no named paying customer, so on our own rubric this cannot sit level with Chutes (90) which has $4.76M of confirmed revenue; the GitHub repo has no LICENSE file, which is a genuine risk for anyone building on it; and the repo was last pushed 2026-07-29, so development has been quiet for around three weeks.",
    active_users: "A real, live, priced product exists \u2014 public per-token pricing verified directly from the API at https://api.engy.ai/v1/models and https://engy.ai/pricing (\"Per-token, pay as you go. No subscriptions and no minimums\") \u2014 so they are actively selling. But zero usage or revenue disclosure: no public token-volume dashboard, no OpenRouter listing found for Engy, no ARR statement, no customer logos, no funding announcement. I deliberately did NOT estimate: with prices spanning $0.045 to $7.50 per 1M tokens and no disclosed volume whatsoever, any arithmetic would be invention rather than derivation.",
