    vs_provider: "MDClone / Syntegra-style synthetic health-data vendors; also Truveta and Illumina's data arms",
    cost_saving_pct: 0,
    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "NIOME (Bittensor SN55, operated by Genomes.io) is a decentralised generator of synthetic genomic data. A central Genomes.io backend (niome-api.genomes.io) issues numbered scientific 'challenges'; miners generate synthetic genomic/CRISPR experiment designs and upload them to validator S3 buckets; validators run a five-stage pipeline (structural gate, statistical plausibility, biophysical simulation, cross-consistency ML, distributional fidelity) and score them. Challenge 1 (CFTR multi-variant haplotype) is complete and Challenge 2 (HBB + CRISPR editing outcome) is live now, with 22 further challenges listed as queued. What is verifiably live: the subnet, the scoring backend (I pulled real per-miner score JSON from /api/v3/miners/scores showing 250-experiment submissions with consistency and distribution-fidelity factors), and an actively developed validator/miner repo. What is roadmap: everything commercial. The intended buyer is pharma/biotech wanting privacy-safe research cohorts, but there is no self-serve product, no dataset catalogue, no pricing, and miner documentation is still marked 'coming soon' on the website. AUDIT: Open source under MIT (github.com/genomesio/subnet-niome-v3), but decentralisation is weak. The backend is the single point of failure and the single point of truth: validators fetch challenge specs from niome-api.genomes.io and the site's own description has the backend generating tasks and the score API served from Genomes.io infrastructure. One contributor only ('jgenome', 39 commits on the v3 repo since 2026-07-13); no second maintainer, no bus-factor mitigation. The repo is largely the standard Bittensor subnet template (docs/stream_tutorial/, running_on_staging.md, subnet_links.py all carry Yuma Rao's 2023 copyright) with roughly 35KB of genuinely original genomics validation code across stage12/stage3/stage4/stage5. No third-party security audit, no scientific peer review, and no external validation of the synthetic data's fidelity \u2014 the claims are self-graded by the operator's own validators. No treasury contract or on-chain governance. Discord is real and reasonably sized (1,923 members, 75 online at check). CAVEATS: I could not verify a single external user of NIOME output \u2014 no pharma partner, no published dataset, no paper citing it. The scientific claim is the weak point for a buyer: 'privacy-safe synthetic genomes' is only useful if the synthetic data demonstrably preserves the statistical structure researchers need, and nobody outside the team has tested that. The 24-challenge roadmap is aspirational marketing \u2014 1 done, 1 running, 22 queued after ~4 months of operation implies a decade at current pace. The site's 'Backed By Industry Leaders' section is testimonial quotes from the Genomes.io CEO himself plus two TAO-ecosystem figures (General TAO Ventures, Yuma), not customers. Genomes.io the parent does sell consumer DNA kits and likely has some revenue, but I found no figure and none of it is attributable to SN55. The existing AlphaGap score of 58 is too generous; nothing has commercially validated since the 2026-07-21 review.",
    active_users: "https://niome.genomes.io/ \u2014 the entire enterprise funnel is a contact form: 'Enterprise projects begin with a scoping discussion covering the research objective, cohort design, validation criteria, export format, and integration requirements. Contact the research team.' No pricing page, no customer names, no disclosed contracts anywhere on the NIOME or Genomes.io sites. Investor/partner logos (Pantera Capital, General TAO Ventures, AMD, Consensys) are for the parent company Genomes.io, not NIOME customers, and none is a revenue disclosure.",
