    benchmark_summary: "Run by Innerworks, a UK security company (CEO Oliver Quie, CTO Oscar Hayek, CPO Tom Ryan, board member Tony Pepper, co-founder of Egress). Miners compete on adversarial cybersecurity challenges \u2014 currently two are live, ab_sniffer (automated browser sniffer, v6) and bot_virus (v1), with five retired (flowradar, flowprint, historical_fingerprinter, dev_fingerprinter, ada_detection, humanize_behaviour). Miners submit encrypted code, validators decrypt and evaluate in sandboxes after a 24h delay, scoring is comparative against the previous best with a 14-day linear decay and a similarity check that rejects plagiarised or resubmitted work. This is the most actively developed of the four by a wide margin: 29 contributors, version 4.9.6 shipped 2026-08-11, commits on the day of research. On chain: 60 miners earning incentive, 14 validators. The commercial story is that outputs harden Innerworks' own bot-detection / device-fingerprinting / true-location product, which Innerworks describes as its 'adversarial R&D system' and 'self-healing system'. AUDIT: MIT licensed and genuinely open (RedTeamSubnet org: RedTeam, miner, validator, rest-scoring-api, challenge repos), with the healthiest contributor base of the four \u2014 29 contributors including outside Bittensor names (unconst, steffencruz, ifrit98, NicheTensor). No third-party security audit published, which is a notable gap for a company selling security. The dominant centralisation risk is stated by the team themselves: RedTeamSubnet/rest-scoring-api is described in its own repo as 'a specialized validator node that provides centralized scoring for the RedTeam Subnet (netuid 61)'. Scoring \u2014 the thing that decides emission \u2014 is therefore a team-operated service, a clean single point of failure and a censorship vector. Challenge definition, versioning and retirement are also entirely team-controlled. Owner coldkey and hotkey are the same key (5ECEsYL82fbXx9KfTZy7G2KXSurnScciJyAVMHZjekc8jUbn), which is poor key hygiene. The project has no dedicated Discord server \u2014 its registered 'discord' is a channel inside the main Bittensor server. CAVEATS: I set revenue to unknown rather than pre-revenue deliberately, and the distinction matters. Innerworks is a real commercial security vendor and its site claims scale \u2014 '$Xb+ assets under protection', 'Xm+ daily active users protected', '243 live ethical hackers' \u2014 but those figures are JavaScript count-up animations whose target values I could not extract, so I cannot cite a single number, and I will not report an unverified one. There is no pricing page (innerworks.me/pricing 404s), no named customer, no disclosed ARR, and no funding announcement I could reach that states revenue. So Innerworks may well have real revenue; the SUBNET almost certainly has none of its own, and none of Innerworks' commercial income is publicly attributed to subnet outputs. Treat the 'integrated into real-world products' claim as unproven \u2014 it is asserted in the README and on innerworks.me but never demonstrated with a shipped feature, changelog or customer. Also note the project's own comms are stale: the RedTeam Medium blog's last posts are from January 2025, including 'Unlocking Value: Monetising RedTeam's Outputs and Challenges', which is forward-looking intent, not evidence of income. Registration cost has collapsed to near the minimum burn (0.00093 TAO), signalling weak demand to mine. I nudged the prior 65 to 68 on the strength of verified daily engineering and a credible commercial parent, but I will not go into the 70s without one disclosed customer or figure. Note: my web-search budget was exhausted mid-task, so I could not chase Innerworks funding rounds or podcast statements as hard as I wanted \u2014 an unresolved revenue thread worth revisiting.",
