    cost_saving_pct: 0,
    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Enigma (SN63, qBitTensor Labs) is a bounty platform for deep-tech challenges, rebranded from 'Quantum Innovate' in April 2026 \u2014 the AlphaGap database still carries the stale name. Researchers submit working solutions (source code, not just answers) to funded milestones; the first valid solution drains the prize and the code is open-sourced as the starting point for the next, harder milestone. Two challenges are live, each with a named external partner: Breaking RSA (with Terra Quantum) and Hardening Quantum Proof (with BlueQubit). I pulled the live platform API and confirmed real activity: Breaking RSA has cleared 340-bit, 460-bit and 480-bit milestones and 500-bit is open with 26 submissions; Hardening Quantum Proof cleared Levels 1 and 2 with Level 3 open at 14 submissions. Milestone prizes are $10,000 rising to $17,500. Unusually for Bittensor, all miner emissions are routed into an on-chain Treasury smart contract that funds those prizes rather than paying miners continuously \u2014 miners compete for discrete bounties and pay a per-submission TAO fee to enter. AUDIT: Strongest governance posture of the four by a distance. MIT licensed (github.com/qbittensor-labs/enigma). The prize treasury is an EVM smart contract on Bittensor with published addresses (vault 0xB291C87759E2BAf678734C45A44121091d999220, governor 0x41a1BE0a7408717877DE25e2c62c2Fb71a04D8A9), 60% success threshold, 50% quorum, ~3-day voting period and 24h timelock, with the team's admin key able only to create and cancel proposals, never approve \u2014 funds cannot leave without validator vote. Contract source, tests and deploy tooling are published in treasury/, and the treasury was itself the subject of an unclaimed bug bounty ('Breaking Treasury Wallets', closed 2026-05-21) \u2014 that is a real adversarial test, though it is not a paid third-party audit by a named firm. Weaknesses: a single code contributor ('ShorShot', 31 commits) and the last push was 2026-07-17, ~4 weeks stale at time of writing. Challenge issuance, submission intake and fee collection all run through centralised qBitTensor infrastructure (challenges./telemetry./tensorauth.qbittensorlabs.com); the platform assigns the fee recipient address. Validators need an RTX PRO 6000 (96GB VRAM) which materially limits who can validate. A mainnet gas bug briefly froze the treasury wallets in July per the team's own broadcast notes. CAVEATS: My $18k figure is a run-rate extrapolation from 69 days of a bounty platform whose submission volume is lumpy by design \u2014 a single hard milestone can sit unsolved for weeks with low submissions, so do not treat it as a stable ARR. More importantly, I could NOT verify where the fee actually lands: the miner CLI transfers TAO to a platform-supplied 'fee recipient' ss58 and neither the repo nor the site discloses whether that is qBitTensor Labs revenue or a treasury top-up. If it routes to treasury, company revenue is $0. Treat the number as platform fee capture, not proven company income. The site says 'qBitTensor Labs retains commercial licensing rights' to breakthroughs but no licence has been sold. The 'Total Prize Pool' counters on the website render blank/dashes, so the live pool is unverifiable from the public page. Both partner relationships (Terra Quantum, BlueQubit) appear to be collaboration/branding, not paid contracts. Small community (Discord 481 members). Note the RSA milestones are cryptographically unremarkable \u2014 500-bit RSA is far below the 829-bit factoring record and nowhere near production RSA-2048 \u2014 so the 'breaking RSA' framing overstates the security significance. Existing AlphaGap record of $0 revenue and score 52 both need updating, and the name 'Quantum Innovate' in the database is 4 months stale.",
    active_users: "Derived from the live platform API https://challenges.qbittensorlabs.com/v1/challenges and per-challenge detail endpoints, which disclose both entry price and submission counts per milestone. Paid submissions: Breaking RSA 4+59+33+26=122; Hardening Quantum Proof 10+25+14=49; all at priceTao 0.1 (priceUsd 19.965). Mock Challenge 17 at 0.01 TAO. Total = 17.27 TAO = ~$3,448 collected since the first milestone opened 2026-06-04, i.e. 69 days. Annualised: 3448 / 69 * 365 = ~$18,240. Prize pools themselves are NOT revenue \u2014 they are funded by subnet emissions into the treasury contract, disclosed at $15,000 total as of the 2026-07-23 broadcast (https://www.qbittensorlabs.com/news). [REVISED ON VERIFICATION: Source opened and verified live. The underlying data is real, current, and about the right entity: challenges.qbittensorlabs.com/v1/challenges plus the four detail endpoints return exactly the cited submission_counts (Breaking RSA 4/59/33/26=122; Hardening Quantum Proof 10/25/14=49; Mock Challenge 17), all at priceTao 0.1 (priceUsd now 19.922 vs 19.965 cited, TAO drift). Subnet identity confirmed: the 480-bit RSA payout is an on-chain transfer_stake with origin_netuid 63 / destination_netuid 63, and qbittensorlabs.com/news names \"Enigma\" and \"Subnet 63\". The claim is also correct to exclude prize pools, which the news page attributes to owner emissions.\n\nThe claim nonetheless fails as an annual revenue figure for three independent reasons.\n\n(1) Fee destination is undisclosed. The API exposes a priceTao field, but nothing in any cited source states that this money accrues to qBitTensor Labs as revenue. The homepage, the news page, and /rules and /treasury (both 404) are silent on submission fees and their destination. On Bittensor subnets, entry fees are commonly burned or recycled into the prize pool rather than booked by the operator. The single load-bearing assumption that converts this API data into \"revenue\" is unsourced.\n\n(2) submission_count is not documented as a paid-submission count. The claim relabels it \"Paid submissions\"; the field is just a count and may include free, invalid, retried, or duplicate submissions. The pricing model (per-attempt vs per-account vs per-milestone) is not disclosed anywhere.\n\n(3) The annualisation is a launch burst extrapolated 5.3x. The first paid milestone opened 2026-06-04, so there is no full cycle to annualise. Milestone cadence is irregular (Breaking Treasury Wallets has priceTao 0 and 0 submissions), and per-milestone counts are trending down, not up (RSA: 4, 59, 33, 26). $18,240/yr is more than five times the total money the platform has collected in its entire history.\n\nEven granting assumption (1) in full, the defensible number is ~$3,440 gross fees collected lifetime, not $18,000 annual. Because it cannot be established that any of that flow is subnet revenue rather than burn or prize-pool funding, and there is a genuine paid-transaction flow so this is clearly not a pre-revenue project either, the correct answer is unknown rather than a figure.]",
    annual_revenue_usd: 0,
    revenue_confidence: "unknown",
