    vs_provider: "None directly; conceptually competes with Renaissance/Epoch-style math prize programs, AlphaProof-type internal research, and Kaggle-style bounty platforms",
    cost_saving_pct: 0,
    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Conjectures.io publishes open mathematical conjectures (mostly Erdos problems and Green's open problems) as exact Lean 4 statements taken from the google-deepmind/formal-conjectures corpus, and pays alpha bounties for submissions whose Lean proof the kernel accepts. It is genuinely live: the public Results page showed 13 submissions on 5-6 Aug 2026, 9 Lean-verified and 4 kernel-rejected, with bounties from ~$668 to ~$2,691 (quoted in both USD and alpha) and several marked 'Reward Paid'. Submission is via a CLI with a signed hotkey plus a flat on-chain fee per attempt that acts purely as a spam gate; deposits are still confirmed by hand because on-chain deposit reading is not built yet, and payouts are signed manually. Bounties are funded from accumulated miner emissions held in a treasury, not from customers. The stated monetization - institutions paying to place a problem in the pool, with revenue flowing to buybacks - is explicitly on the roadmap under 'Now/Next', not live, as is the second track (paid verified software-optimisation bounties). Buyers today: none. AUDIT: Materially closed. The validator, task generator, scoring and submission API are explicitly withheld - the public miner harness README states 'Production submissions are private' and instructs miners to keep 'validator systems, task generators, scoring, wallets, and infrastructure out of this repo'. The only fully open component is the miner starter harness (unarbos/ninja) and the problem statements, which are Google DeepMind's, not theirs. Kernel acceptance is objective and reproducible, which is a real strength, but the second gate is discretionary: a human decides 'certification', and the Results page shows that human rejecting verified proofs ('Already solved before this submission', 'Duplicate', 'Defect in the published task') and issuing 'Partial award'. Payouts are signed by hand from an operator-controlled treasury. No third-party audit. Single point of failure: the operator (the 'Arbos'/unarbos account, which also runs SN97 Albedo, SN3 Teutonic and has commit rights on the SN120 Affine repo) controls problem selection, bounty sizing, review outcome and the treasury keys. CAVEATS: Blunt version: this subnet has changed identity three times in five months. The same operator's repos show SN66 as 'Eclair - Image-to-Video Generation Subnet' (Mar 2026), then 'Subnet 66 Validator - King of the Hill agent evaluation' (Apr 2026), then the Lean-proof market (Aug 2026); third-party listings still describe SN66 as 'distributed software engineering evaluation for AI agents'. Treat the current thesis as roughly one month old. I could not verify: the flat submission fee amount, the total number of submissions ever (only 13 are shown), whether any of the 9 verified proofs actually settled a previously open problem (the site itself concedes several were 'Already solved before this submission' or exploited a 'Defect in the published task'), who the legal/operating entity is, or any X account. Overstated on their own materials: the home page leads with two conjectures that fell in July 2026, and the site itself admits neither came from this platform - that is borrowed credibility, not traction. The dollar figures on the Results page are alpha bounties converted at spot, i.e. emissions recycled to miners, and must not be read as revenue.",
    active_users: "https://conjectures.io/roadmap - the 'Monetization' block ('Buyer: Problem sponsors ... Revenue Flows into buybacks') sits under the roadmap, and the FAQ states 'The intended model is that an institution pays the subnet to place its statement in the pool.' No sponsor, no paying customer, and no revenue figure appears anywhere on the site. The only inbound cash flow is the flat per-submission anti-spam fee, whose amount is not published (FAQ: 'The exact price is shown there rather than here, because it is a configuration value and can change') and which at 13 lifetime submissions is immaterial.",
