    subnet_id: 69,
    subnet_name: "Herald",
    benchmark_score: 32,
    benchmark_category: "Data",
    vs_provider: "Traditional PR agencies and media-outreach platforms \u2014 Cision, Muck Rack, PRNewswire/Business Wire",
    cost_saving_pct: 0,
    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Herald is a subnet for verified mainstream-media placement \u2014 effectively an incentivised PR network. Miners are PR operators and outlet owners who get an article published in a real news outlet and submit the URL; validators run a code-only public-web verification oracle over each claim, checking in cheapest-first order that the on-chain commitment is valid, that the outlet is in a signed tier registry, that the URL is live, that the piece is genuine editorial rather than paid placement, that it matches the brief's topic, and that it appears in a search index. Attribution uses commit-reveal \u2014 a miner commits a salted hash of (brief, outlet, hotkey, nonce) before publication so the target outlet stays hidden, and the earliest valid commit wins each article, one paid placement per outlet-brief pair. Rewards vest in installments over a 30-day persistence window and are clawed back with the hotkey slashed if the article disappears. The engineering is real and reasonably deep (roughly 214 files, a signed 78-outlet tier-3 registry, vesting, slashing, quorum, fail-closed production startup, Semgrep SAST in CI). What is NOT live is the network: the public dashboard at heraldmedia.ai reports live network data unavailable, and on-chain the subnet shows zero current emission and a single active miner. Two revenue models are described \u2014 client-funded briefs where a customer commissions coverage, and a standing brief paid from emissions \u2014 but only the emission-funded side has any mechanism behind it today. AUDIT: No third-party security audit. Genuinely better hygiene than most at this stage: MIT licensed, a Semgrep SAST workflow in CI, and a fail-closed production mode where neuron startup aborts on a non-mainnet scope, simulator or local endpoints, unsigned feeds or registries, missing provider credentials, consensus-fingerprint drift, or a missing live registry anchor. Against that, the centralisation is structural and material. First, the outlet registry is an ed25519-signed artefact produced by the team (HERALD_REGISTRY_PUBKEY, herald/registry/admin.py): the owner decides unilaterally which outlets count and what tier they get, and outlet tier is a direct multiplier on miner pay \u2014 that is centralised control of the reward function itself. Second, production runs on a standalone closed-source 'herald-backend' with Postgres and admin/results/reveals/disputes/funding tokens; the open-sourced JSON brief board is dev-only, so the component that actually issues briefs and holds funds is not public. Third, the verification oracle depends on paid third-party APIs \u2014 SerpAPI, Brave Search, ScrapingBee \u2014 and the enabled provider set is a consensus parameter that must match fleet-wide, so an expired API key or a provider outage does not merely degrade the subnet, it can fork validator consensus. Fourth, single anonymous contributor: helloherald69 has all 125 commits, the HeraldMedia org (created 2026-06-29) has no name, blog, email or linked X, and no team member is identified anywhere. On-chain owner coldkey 5HBPFVcJxuhT6PFg93s5B5qa9GZjMQWRBWWEssZMDAMmRAvc, registered 2026-05-08 for ~2,632 TAO. CAVEATS: I could not verify that a single article has ever been placed and paid through Herald \u2014 no leaderboard, no placement log, no dashboard data, one active miner on chain. Everything about the product is currently code and copy. Development has also gone quiet: the last commit was 2026-07-28, roughly two weeks before this research, after a burst in July; that is not yet abandonment but it is not the cadence of a launching network either. The pitch has an inherent credibility problem a buyer should weigh: the oracle claims to distinguish 'real news (not paid)' from paid placement while the entire subnet exists to pay people to obtain placements \u2014 I am sceptical that check survives contact with adversarial miners and sponsored-content grey areas, and there is no published evaluation showing it does. The $32,000 figure on the site is illustrative and should not be recorded anywhere as revenue or as a booked brief. The supplied name 'Herald' is correct and the netuid binding is confirmed in code (NETUID=69, HERALD_PRODUCTION_NETUID=69).",
    active_users: "https://heraldmedia.ai \u2014 the site's own dashboard shows 'live network data unavailable' and names no client. The $32,000 brief reward pool shown on the site is explicitly framed as an illustrative pilot example, not a booked customer, and the client-funded brief model is described as a design, not as something transacting. Corroborated on chain at https://api.taostats.io/api/subnet/latest/v1?netuid=69 (block 8830473, 2026-08-12): emission '0', projected_emission 0.0229, active_miners: 1 against 7 validators \u2014 a network with one miner is placing no articles and therefore billing no one. No pricing page for brands exists.",
