    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Compelle runs an adversarial-persuasion mechanism: miners commit written debate strategies on-chain, and each epoch the reference validator pairs them in Pro/Con LLM debates on a shared motion, scores outcomes via Elo, and sets weights. The consumer product at compelle.com is genuinely live and transactable today \u2014 you submit a claim, two of the subnet's miners argue it, an independent multi-family judge panel rules, and the verdict, full transcript and a plain-language writeup are emailed to you. Checkout is open with card payment, priced live off actual compute: ~$1.27 for a single debate, from ~$11 for a full miner tournament (Swiss or round-robin). Around it sits a large content surface (blog, podcast with an RSS feed, course, glossary, technique library, public debate corpus, live status page). The chain side is verifiably running: the public API returned 358,253 indexed games and 1,204 miners at epoch block 8,828,778 when checked on 2026-08-12. IMPORTANT: this slot was previously the Hermes GraphQL data-query subnet (SubQuery/Yuma, launched 15 Jan 2026); Compelle is a full replacement of the netuid's purpose, not a rename of the same product. AUDIT: Only the validator is open source: https://github.com/compelle/compelle-validator, MIT, created 2026-05-03, ~100 commits, last push 2026-08-06, ONE contributor under the pseudonymous account \"compelle\", 2 stars, 0 forks. The code is bespoke real work (Elo tournament engine, king-of-the-hill vacancy machinery, Watchtower auto-update with optional GPG signature verification, pinned bittensor==10.5.0), not a template fork \u2014 but the miner side and the compelle.com backend/payment stack are NOT published. Operator is fully anonymous: no team page, no company entity, and no X account or Discord link anywhere on the site or in the repo (verified by extracting every href from the homepage). No third-party audit. Two clear single points of failure: (1) all site data and the public API are served from one operator-run indexer, and (2) validator LLM inference depends on the external Chutes API \u2014 live game records already show failed debates with reason \"LLM error: provider unavailable\". Validator auto-update pulls main branch every 10 minutes, so the anonymous maintainer can push code to every validator that runs the reference client. CAVEATS: Could not verify: who operates it (fully anonymous), whether any paying customer has ever completed a checkout, any revenue figure, or any funding. Could not confirm the handover mechanics or date from Hermes to Compelle on this netuid. taostats now displays the netuid as Compelle, so the supplied name is current \u2014 but stale third-party pages (backprop.finance, some search indexes) still label SN82 \"Hermes\", which will corrupt any scraper keyed on name. Overstated on their own materials: the homepage stats banner presents subnet-internal debate counts in a way a reader will naturally read as customer traction; subnetalpha.ai's profile of this subnet is stale and wrong (it says the subnet is a generic 'data' subnet with no public repo and no product).",
    active_users: "Live card checkout exists at https://compelle.com/custom.html (\"Checkout is open... pay by card\") at ~$1.27/debate and ~$11+/tournament, so revenue is plausibly non-zero, but no figure, order count, or paid-usage counter is disclosed anywhere. CRITICAL TRAP: the homepage \"102,389 debates judged / 2,997 tournaments\" and the API's larger live counts (https://compelle.com/api/stats -> {\"tournaments\":6994,\"debates\":358250}) are subnet-INTERNAL validator-run miner-vs-miner debates, not customer orders. The per-game records at https://compelle.com/api/games contain only validator_hotkey/pro_hotkey/con_hotkey fields with no customer or payment marker. Any revenue estimate multiplying those counts by $1.27 would be wrong by orders of magnitude. I did not derive one.",
    annual_revenue_usd: 0,
    revenue_confidence: "unknown",
    revenue_evidence: "Live card checkout exists at https://compelle.com/custom.html (\"Checkout is open... pay by card\") at ~$1.27/debate and ~$11+/tournament, so revenue is plausibly non-zero, but no figure, order count, or paid-usage counter is disclosed anywhere. CRITICAL TRAP: the homepage \"102,389 debates judged / 2,997 tournaments\" and the API's larger live counts (https://compelle.com/api/stats -> {\"tournaments\":6994,\"debates\":358250}) are subnet-INTERNAL validator-run miner-vs-miner debates, not customer orders. The per-game records at https://compelle.com/api/games contain only validator_hotkey/pro_hotkey/con_hotkey fields with no customer or payment marker. Any revenue estimate multiplying those counts by $1.27 would be wrong by orders of magnitude. I did not derive one.",
    last_updated: "2026-08-12",
    sources: ["https://compelle.com/", "https://compelle.com/custom.html", "https://compelle.com/method.html", "https://compelle.com/about.html", "https://compelle.com/status.html", "https://compelle.com/api/stats", "https://compelle.com/api/health", "https://compelle.com/api/games?limit=1", "https://github.com/compelle/compelle-validator", "https://taostats.io/subnets/82/chart", "https://compelle.com"],
