    subnet_id: 93,
    subnet_name: "Bitcast",
    benchmark_score: 84,
    benchmark_category: "Data",
    vs_provider: "Grin / Aspire / Whalar and traditional influencer-marketing agencies; structurally, YouTube BrandConnect",
    cost_saving_pct: 65,
    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "Bitcast is performance-based creator marketing run as a subnet. Brands publish 'briefs' (content requirements plus a boost multiplier); creators mine by publishing YouTube videos or X posts that satisfy a brief; validators take temporary OAuth tokens against the creator's YouTube Analytics to verify real watch-time and engagement, and rewards scale off YouTube Premium revenue statistics. Format types are priced differently \u2014 a Dedicated video (sponsor topic is >=80% of the video, capped at 2 rewarded videos per account per brief) earns full reward, an Ad-Read segment earns 20%. Brands pay a flat fee per campaign (10 TAO historically), and that fiat/TAO inflow is used to buy back and burn SN93 alpha rather than being paid straight to creators \u2014 creators are paid from emissions. Live and measurably working: the protocol dashboard at bitcast.network, a no-code miner, both YouTube and X mechanisms, and a creator portal. Note the creator portal now redirects from creator.bitcast.network to creator.outfold.ai \u2014 'Outfold' ('YouTube creator marketing, built to scale') appears to be a commercial front-end being spun up alongside the subnet. AUDIT: Open source under MIT. Two contributors only \u2014 mizu-tx at 83% (113 commits) and elliot-bitcast at 17% \u2014 which is a genuine key-man risk for a subnet handling this much value. Development is current but slowing (12 commits in the 90 days to mid-August 2026). The most important centralisation fact: validators must obtain OAuth tokens to read creators' private YouTube Analytics, so the reward oracle depends on both Google's API and on validators being trusted with creator account access \u2014 a privacy and collusion surface with no cryptographic mitigation. The briefs server is centrally hosted by the Bitcast team, meaning the team unilaterally decides which briefs exist and what boost multiplier each carries, i.e. the team steers where emissions flow. A commit merged 2026-08-08 ('divert excess emissions from burn UID to treasury UID 155') routes surplus emissions to a team-controlled treasury. Bitcast runs no Discord server of its own \u2014 support lives in a channel inside the main Bittensor Discord. No third-party audit of the code or of the dashboard's revenue accounting. CAVEATS: The revenue is real but I could not establish how much of it is arm's-length. Every source I found describes the advertisers as Bittensor-ecosystem participants and early campaigns as internally generated by Bitcast and ecosystem stakeholders; not one external consumer brand is named anywhere public, and the roadmap item 'open to crypto brands beyond the Bittensor ecosystem' was a Q4 2025 plan I could not confirm shipped. If TAO-denominated ecosystem sponsors are paying TAO to buy back TAO-ecosystem alpha, a meaningful fraction of this $341K is circular rather than external demand. I could not build a time-series to verify my annualisation: Wayback has no archived snapshot of the current dashboard design, so the monetisation start date is inferred, and the whole $341.1K figure is self-reported by the team on their own page with no independent attestation. One number that undercuts the bull case: lifetime rewards PAID ($489.1K) exceed lifetime REVENUE ($341.1K), so emissions are still subsidising the network \u2014 which sits awkwardly against third-party reports (Stillcore Capital, 'State of Subnets July 26') calling SN93 'subnet-profitable'. The Outfold rebrand is unexplained and unannounced; I could not determine whether Outfold is the same legal entity, and value migrating from the subnet to a separate commercial vehicle is a risk holders should price. Score of 84 essentially confirms the existing 85; it does not reach 90+ because the revenue is small, partly related-party, and has no named external customers.",
    active_users: "https://bitcast.network/ \u2014 the team's own protocol dashboard, read 2026-08-12, marked 'LIVE / UPDATED 2026-08-12', displays: REVENUE $341.1K, BUYBACKS $197.1K (57.8% recycling ratio), PAID $489.1K lifetime (133.4K alpha), 5,678 content pieces rewarded, 973 active creators (78 YouTube, 895 X), 10.97M combined audience, 4.09M 3-day views. ARITHMETIC: the $341.1K is LIFETIME cumulative, not annual. Monetisation appears to have started around Q3 2025 (subnetalpha.ai records roughly 40-50 TAO/month of campaign revenue as of late 2025, ~$14-20k/month at then-prevailing TAO prices), giving roughly 13 months of monetised operation to 2026-08-12. $341,100 / 13 months = ~$26.2K/month = ~$315K annualised. Because the start date is inferred rather than disclosed I round down to $300,000. Revenue is trending up, so trailing-twelve-months is likely at or slightly above this figure rather than below it. [REVISED ON VERIFICATION: Source verified live: bitcast.network (updated 2026-08-12) does display REVENUE $341.1K, BUYBACKS $197.1K (57.8%), PAID $489.1K / 133.4K alpha lifetime, 5,678 content pieces, 973 creators. This is genuine revenue (brands paying for creator content), not TVL, emissions, market cap, or funding \u2014 so the subnet is NOT pre-revenue.\n\nHowever the claimed $300,000 ANNUAL figure does not hold up. The dashboard states no time period whatsoever for the revenue line: I re-queried the page specifically for a start date, \"since\" label, or period marker and found none. The only date is \"UPDATED 2026-08-12\". Notably PAID is explicitly labeled \"lifetime\" while REVENUE carries no label at all.\n\nThe $300K is not on the dashboard and is not derivable from it. It comes from dividing an undated cumulative total by an INFERRED 13-month monetisation window. That Q3-2025 start date is the load-bearing assumption and is disclosed nowhere. Change it and the answer swings widely: netuid 93 has existed roughly 16-18 months (Bittensor registration passed the 90s in early 2025), so if monetisation tracked subnet life the rate is ~$227K/yr; if it truly began Q3 2025, ~$315K; if revenue is back-weighted, TTM could exceed $341K. The cited page cannot distinguish these cases.\n\nAdditional cautions: the dashboard is the team's own self-reported marketing surface with no methodology or audit, and PAID ($489.1K) exceeding REVENUE ($341.1K) confirms payouts are substantially emissions-funded, so the revenue line overstates operating scale if read as a business.\n\nI could not corroborate a monetisation start date from a second source (subnetalpha.ai/subnet/93 returns 404, docs.bitcast.network does not resolve, web-search budget exhausted), leaving the pivotal assumption unverified.\n\nCorrected figure of $227,000 is a conservative floor: $341.1K lifetime spread over the maximum plausible subnet lifespan (~18 months), annualised. This is anchored on a bounded subnet age rather than an invented start date. Marked \"estimated\" because real revenue exists and is citable, but the annual rate is an inference, not a stated figure.]",
