    perf_delta: "Researched 2026-08-12. Active.",
    benchmark_summary: "A pitched no-code platform for deploying, managing and monetising Bittensor miners \u2014 one-click miner launches across subnets, Polkadot wallet auth, real-time earnings and health monitoring, claimed support for 32+ subnets, and a marketplace for community-built miner code. Its self-description is 'Democratizing Bittensor'. Critically, none of this is live. The website is a waitlist funnel with repeated Join Waitlist calls to action and no product access. The dashboard figures shown on the page ('Deploying miner to Subnet 122... done, Status: active, Rewards: 0.42 TAO/day') are an illustrative mockup, not live data. The GitHub organisation contains exactly two repositories: 'assets' (a favicon, a logo.svg and a logo-circle.png) and 'Incentive-Mechanism' (a single 194KB file named CookingTAO_IM_V1___Draft.pdf). There is no miner code, no validator code, and no protocol implementation published anywhere. This netuid previously hosted Bitrecs, a recommendation-engine subnet; CookingTAO is a new occupant, not a rename. AUDIT: Effectively nothing to audit, and that is itself the finding. No open-source miner, validator or protocol code exists in the GitHub organisation. The only technical artefact is an unversioned draft PDF ('V1___Draft') of an incentive mechanism, last pushed 30 June 2026. No named team members anywhere on the site or the GitHub org. No third-party audit. The proposed product is a custodial-adjacent one \u2014 it would deploy and manage miners on users' behalf with wallet integration \u2014 which is precisely the category where anonymous teams and closed source are most dangerous, since it implies handling or signing with user wallet credentials. The org has 2 followers and was created 29 September 2025, updated 22 May 2026. Anyone considering this must treat it as an unaudited, unimplemented, anonymous-team proposal. CAVEATS: This subnet was NEVER BENCHMARKED and my blunt read is that there is no evidence of a product, which is why it scores in the sub-30 band. I set is_active true rather than false on a narrow technicality: it holds netuid 122, the site is up, and the repos saw pushes in late June and early July 2026, so it is not deregistered or clearly abandoned. But it is functionally indistinguishable from a placeholder, and a reasonable analyst could set is_active false. The claim of '32+ active subnets' supported is, in my view, materially overstated \u2014 there is no published code that supports any subnet. The '0.42 TAO/day' figure on their site is a mockup and must never be treated as a performance datum. I could not read the draft incentive-mechanism PDF's contents, only its existence and size. The X handle @CookingTao_ is taken from the verified twitter_username field on their GitHub organisation record, but I could not open the account itself because the WebSearch budget was exhausted, so I have not seen their posting cadence or any founder statements.",
    active_users: "https://cookingtao.com \u2014 the entire site is gated behind Join Waitlist CTAs with no product access, no pricing, no fee schedule and no revenue-share terms disclosed. A product that has not shipped and cannot be signed up for cannot have revenue. Corroborated by https://github.com/CookingTao, whose only two repos are a logo asset folder and a draft incentive-mechanism PDF \u2014 there is no implementation to monetise.",
