    benchmark_summary: "ByteLeap is a GPU cloud that uses Bittensor SN128 to aggregate third-party hardware into its supply. It runs a three-tier stack: validators coordinate and score, miners aggregate up to 100 workers each and interface with the chain, and workers run the actual compute (libvirt/VM gateway sessions plus CPU/GPU matrix-multiply challenges verified by a two-phase merkle commit-then-sample protocol). The demand side is a conventional centralised product \u2014 a storefront at byteleap.ai backed by a private API at host-api.byteleap.ai/podsail/api with email signup, signin, wallet and costs pages. The catalogue is live and real: nine GPU families (RTX 3090/4090/5090, A100-SXM4, HGX H100, HGX H200, HGX B200, RTX PRO6000) each offered as bare metal or VM, priced hourly through yearly. Miner incentive is explicitly tied to that storefront: 'Lease Revenue (100%)' is the primary score component and idle workers score zero, so emissions follow real paid rentals. The critical unknown is whether any rentals exist \u2014 every one of the 18 SKUs returns stock: 0. AUDIT: Code is open source under MIT across three repos (byteleap-Miner, byteleap-Validator, byteleap-Worker), which is better than most compute subnets \u2014 the validator's scoring and the miner's challenge protocol are both inspectable. But the decentralisation posture is weak and the operator is opaque. The scoring input that dominates rewards (lease revenue) is read from ByteLeap's own closed marketplace API, so the subnet owner unilaterally determines almost the entire reward vector; miners cannot verify their own lease scores against any independent source. The validator README also documents 'VM Gateway Enrollment Brokering: retrieves, caches and serves VM gateway enroll tokens from MeshHub to miners' \u2014 a proprietary, centrally controlled enrollment dependency and a hard single point of failure. Validator config includes an operator-managed validator whitelist ('ops: Optimize the whitelist of validators'). The GitHub account byteleapai is a personal User account, not an Organization, with no name, no bio, no blog, and it is the sole contributor to all repos (14 commits on the miner, 9 on the validator). No company entity, no named team, no legal or jurisdiction disclosure, and no third-party security audit found. Development cadence is a concern: after a burst in late 2025 the validator went untouched from 2026-01-20 to 2026-08-11, and the miner from 2026-01-12 to 2026-08-11 \u2014 a seven-month gap on both, with the August commits being an SDK version bump and a .DS_Store deletion rather than substantive work. Chain snapshot shows emission 0 with 11 validators and 21 miners. CAVEATS: I could not establish revenue either way, and that ambiguity is the single most important caveat here. ByteLeap is a real priced product \u2014 I pulled the live catalogue directly from their API (POST host-api.byteleap.ai/podsail/api/serversku/specs) and it returns genuine rate cards, e.g. RTX 3090 bare metal $0.15/hr, $108/mo, $1,296/yr; H100 SXM5 bare metal $1.60/hr, $1,152/mo; B200 bare metal $3.50/hr, $2,520/mo. But every single SKU reports stock: 0, and that is genuinely two-sided: it could mean fully rented (implying revenue) or an empty catalogue with no hardware behind it (implying none). I refuse to guess, so I have set revenue to 0 with confidence 'unknown' rather than derive an estimate \u2014 any arithmetic off a rate card with no verified utilisation would be fabrication, and this figure feeds a scoring formula. Do not upgrade this to 'estimated' without a utilisation or occupancy number. I did not create an account to see behind the auth wall. Two data-quality red flags in their own catalogue: the RTX 5090 bare-metal SKUs list a 1-month price of $0.275, identical to the hourly rate, which is an obvious pricing bug sitting in production; and the site advertises B200 and H200 capacity while showing zero stock for them. Their subnet_url is recorded on chain as the bare string 'ByteLeap.ai' without a scheme, and the on-chain Discord link is a channel-deep URL inside the main Bittensor server rather than their own server \u2014 there is no independent ByteLeap Discord I could find. No disclosed customers, no logos, no funding announcement, no usage numbers anywhere. Web search was unavailable this session so press and founder statements are unchecked, and I could not pull @byteleap_ai's timeline.",
